  "opening": "One qubit can hold the answer to a one-bit question, and we have seen interference decide it. Most tasks, however, take many bits as input. The composite-state postulate already tells us what a register of qubits can hold: one amplitude for every bit string, including joint states that are not products of single-qubit states. It does not tell us how a computation acts on such a register.",
  "opening_continuation": [
    "How does a gate on one qubit act on the whole register, and which gate couples two qubits? Coupling lets a circuit create entanglement, so we next ask how a measurement recognizes it. Entanglement then raises two limits, on copying a state and on sending a message, and teleportation shows what a shared entangled pair does allow. Finally the register computes: one reversible evaluation of a function acts on every input at once, and we ask how much of the result a measurement can deliver.",
    "Reading out more than a random sample needs interference between the evaluations, which the next chapter builds from function queries."
  ]
}
